{
  "version": 1,
  "meta": {
    "name": "flan-t5-base",
    "family": "t5",
    "notes": "T5 v1.1 at base size, instruction-tuned: twelve layers a stack, width 768, twelve heads of 64 and a gated GELU feed-forward of 2,048, the GELU taken with its tanh approximation. Each stack has one learned relative-position table, [32 buckets, heads], wired to every layer: a score gets table[bucket(kv - q), h] added, with the bucket exact for small distances and logarithmic out to 128, two-sided in the encoder and one-sided in the decoder. Hugging Face keeps it on the first layer and hands its bias down; here it is a block outside the stack, which is the same parameters read the same way. Cross-attention has none. The attention is unscaled, T5 having folded 1/sqrt(d_kv) into its initialisation, and there are no biases anywhere. The norms are RMSNorm without a mean, as T5's LayerNorm is, with 1e-6 inside the square root. The encoder and the decoder read one embedding table: the decoder's is tied to the encoder's. The default lengths are T5's pretraining: 512 tokens of corrupted input and 114 of target. Unlike the original T5, v1.1 gives the output head a table of its own, which is why its vocabulary is counted twice and there is no rescale before it.",
    "published": {
      "params": 247577856,
      "source": "https://huggingface.co/google/flan-t5-base"
    }
  },
